M etal−organic frameworks (MOFs), which are built linkers, have been intensively explored as promising precursor materials.1 For example, various functional materials, ranging from organic to inorganic materials (metal oxides, metal nitride, metal sulfide, and metal phosphide), have been synthesized by using various MOF precursors, which enable a wide range of applications including catalysis,2−5 capture of gases,6 energy storage,7,8 drug delivery,9 and chemical sensors.10 In particular, microstructures, porosity, and morphologies of MOFs can be easily tailored for desired applications by assembling various types of metal nodes and organic linkers. For instance, Guo et al. developed various morphologies of MIL-53 (Fe-based MOFs) from yolk−shell octahedron to nanorod, by controlling the coordination of Fe ions and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ linkers via microwave-assisted synthesis. Various morphologies of the MIL-53 lead to the formation of high-surface-area ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ architectures after calcination, particularly optimized as high-performance anode materials for lithium ion batteries.11 Tang et al. developed the ultrathin NiCo bimetallic MOFs nanosheets, which exhibited great electrocatalytic activity.12
More recently, for further improvement in the functionalities of MOF-derived materials, the hierarchical MOF architectures have been exploited. Representatively, Guan et al. developed hierarchical Fe−carbon composite materials by templating hybridized dual-MOFs (MIL-88B and ZIF-8; ZIF, zeolitic imidazolate framework),13 exhibiting superior catalytic performance. However, the synthesis of multidimensional hybrid MOFs by a simple one-pot process and their sacrificial templating route for creation of heterogeneous hybrid oxide architectures have rarely been investigated.
Chemiresistive gas sensors have recently attracted much attention due to increasing demands for monitoring hazardous gases including acetone, hydrogen sulfide, toluene, and nitrogen dioxide.14,15 Among the various sensitive sensing materials, noble-metal-catalyst-decorated semiconducting metal oxides (SMOs) have been regarded as the best sensing layers, showing superior sensing performances.16,17 However, in the case of high-temperature-operating SMO gas sensors, metal catalysts are often degraded after long cycled operation, which is detrimental to long-term device stability. As a costeffective and more reliable approach, the heterogeneous hierarchical metal-oxide nanomaterials, which possess high surface area, high porosity, and electronically sensitized multiheterojunction structures, have been intensively exploited as promising sensing layers. However, the simple and massproductive fabrication of such materials is still challenging; thus, a new synthetic approach for the rational designing of multiheterojunction structures with high surface area and open porosity is required.
In this work, we designed hybrid MOFs (HMOFs), i.e., onedimensional (1D) rod-shaped MOFs anchored to twodimensional (2D) sheet-shaped MOFs, as a new sacrificial template for direct synthesis of a hierarchical metal-oxide composite with abundant $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunctions, which are suitable for highly sensitive chemiresistors. Upon the simple control of the relative ratio of two mixed solvents, the ZIF-67 (Co-ion-based MOF) with various morphologies, including polyhedron, sheet, rod, and belt, was successfully synthesized. Furthermore, the in situ coupling of 1D rodlike ZIF-67 and 2D sheetlike ZIF-8 resulted in the creation of daisy-flower-like composite MOF structures. Through simple calcination of the HMOFs, hierarchically assembled 1D ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods functionalized on 2D $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ sheets were formed and used as superior acetone-sensing layers.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Various morphologies of ZIF-67, i.e., polyhedron (ZIF-67), sheets (ZIF-L), rods (ZIF-67 rods), and belts (ZIF-67 belts), are tailored by controlled manipulation of a single solvent and/ or cosolvents used for MOF growth (Figure 1). When the weight ratio of HMIM/Co precursors dispersed in methanol is 2.16, the polyhedral ZIF-67 structure with uniform size distribution of $300{-}400~\mathrm{~nm}$ is generally formed by the assembly of Co ions and 2-methylimidazole (HMIM) organic linkers (Figure $\displaystyle{\mathrm{la-c}})$ , which has been introduced in the previous literature.10 On the other hand, the ZIF-67 assembled in deionized (DI) water exhibits flat leaf-shaped Co-based ZIFL structures (Figure 1d). As shown in the SEM image of Figure 1e and TEM image of Figure 1f, Co-based ZIF-L structures with leaf shapes are well-formed by 2D networking between Co ions and HMIM linkers.18 To further engineer the microstructure and morphology of ZIF-67, we employed the cosolvent system of DI water and ethanol (EtOH) with two different volume ratios $\left(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ , i.e., DI water $\mathrm{{'EtOH}=1}3{:}27$ $(\approx1{:}2)$ and 27:13 $(\approx2{:}1)$ . Interestingly, when the HMIM organic linkers dissolved in EtOH $(27~\mathrm{mL})$ ) are mixed with Co ions dissolved in DI water $\mathrm{i}3\ \mathrm{mL})$ , the rod-shaped ZIF-67 (ZIF-67 rods) was created (Figure 1g). As shown in Figure $^{\mathrm{1h,}}$ the elongated ZIF-67 rods had micron-sized length $(\sim5~\mu\mathrm{m})$ and mean width of $255.83\mathrm{nm}$ . The TEM image of ZIF-67 rods (Figure 1i) exhibits dense rod structures without any macrosized $(>50\ \mathrm{nm})$ pores. On the other hand, in the case of using a mixed solvent with volume ratio of DI water (2)/ EtOH (1), the belt-shaped ZIF-67 (ZIF-67 belts) structures were formed (Figure $^{\mathrm{1j,k}}$ ). Moreover, numerous macrosized pores were created in the inner side of ZIF-67 belts (TEM image of Figure 1l). Our observation revealed that the dimension and shape of ZIF-67 can be tailored by varying the ratio of two mixed solvents even in constant weight ratio of HMIM/Co precursors.
The growth mechanism of ZIF-67 rods can be explained on the basis of (i) different degrees of HMIM deprotonation and (ii) the Marangoni flow effect. First, in the viewpoint of deprotonation of HMIM, when Co ions react with HMIM linkers dispersed in DI water, the outer side of tetragonal $\mathrm{[CoN_{4}]}$ in Co-based ZIF-L consists of one free MIM and three Co−MIM bonds, showing elongated Co-based ZIF-L structures. Thus, DI water tends to restrain the [200] and [020] growth of ZIF-67.18,19 On the other hand, methanol induces the binding of four organic linkers with Co, showing polyhedron structures.18 That is, the different degree of HMIM deprotonation is a critical factor for the binding state of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-}$ MIM bonds. As shown in Table S1 of the Supporting Information, the $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values of solutions used for the growth of ZIF-67 rods $\mathrm{\Phi}_{\cdot\mathrm{PH}}=8.05,\$ and ZIF-67 belts ( $\mathrm{\Phi}^{\mathrm{pH}}=8.22,$ ) were slightly lower than that of DI water $\mathrm{\Phi^{\prime}p H}=8.47,$ ). Considering that DI water with a high $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ value leads to suppressed [200] and [020] growth of ZIF-67, the reduction of $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ level in mixed solution, i.e., DI water $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\prime}\mathrm{EtOH}=1:2$ and 2:1, induces relatively weaker suppression in [020] and [200] growth; this is well-matched with the XRD results of ZIF-67 rods.
Regarding the Marangoni flow effect, when EtOH solution is mixed with DI water (Figure S3 of the Supporting Information), the Marangoni flow can be formed at the interface between DI water and EtOH because of the difference in surface energy $\left(\gamma_{\mathrm{water}},77~\mathrm{mN/m};\right.$ and $\gamma_{\mathrm{EtOH}},22$ $\mathrm{mN/m}\mathrm{,}$ ).20 Because of the creation of a surface tension gradient between DI water and EtOH, the fluidic flow from EtOH to DI water spontaneously occurs, enabling the one-dimensional (1D) alignment of HMIM into the Co-ion-dissolved DI water.
Similarly, Cai et al. reported that the polystyrene nanoparticles can be aligned by the Marangoni flow in the EtOH/water cosolvent system.20 As a control experiment, when both Co nitrate and HMIM powders were simultaneously dissolved in the cosolvents (EtOH and DI water), the conventional polyhedral ZIF-67 structures were formed (Figure S6 of the Supporting Information). These results further support that the formation of ZIF-67 rods and ZIF-67 belt structures is greatly affected by the Marangoni flow effect.
To prepare hierarchically assembled metal-oxide structures with numerous $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunctions, we calcined sequentially grown hybrid MOF templates (HMOFs) at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 3a).21,22 Considering the grain sizes and hierarchical structures, we selected $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as the optimized calcination temperature. It is noted that the lower temperature $(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ as compared to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ cannot induce crystallinity in $\mathrm{ZnO-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structures (Figure S4 of the Supporting Information). On the other hand, using the Scherrer equation, it was observed that higher calcination temperatures, i.e., 550, 650, and $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ resulted in larger grain sizes (53.6, 69.5, and $92.7~\mathrm{\nm},$ respectively), compared with that $\left(31.7~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ of the $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ calcined double- ${\cdot}{\bf C}{\circ}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a\mathbf{)}Z\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet (Table S2 of the Supporting Information), thereby leading to degraded sensitivity to target gas molecules, in accordance with previous studies.23,24 More importantly, a higher calcination temperature resulted in the agglomeration of nanostructures and collapsed hierarchical structures of the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $@$ $\mathrm{znO}$ sheet (Figure S5 of the Supporting Information). Therefore, we chose $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as the optimized calcination temperature for HMOFs to achieve well-developed nanostructures with small grain size. The Co-based ZIF-67 rods were grown as the first seed layer, and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based ZIF-L was subsequently formed as the second layer on the ZIF-67 rods. It is noted that the Zn-based ZIF-L can also be formed by following the synthetic process used for growth of Co-based ZIF-L (Figure S7 of the Supporting Information). Since Znbased ZIF-L-derived thin-layered metal-oxide structures induce the effective modulation of the electron depletion region, they are highly suitable as the chemiresistive-type gas sensing layer.25,26 In the case of using macroporous ZIF-67 belts as the first seed layer, calcined structures collapsed because of weak morphological stability caused by numerous pores (Figure S8 of the Supporting Information) that existed within ZIF-67 belts (Figure S1c of the Supporting Information). Thus, we used ZIF-67 rods with high structural stability as a seed layer for subsequent growth of Zn-based ZIF-L, enabling multidimensionally assembled 1D−2D structures. During the HMOF formation process, the 2D Zn-based ZIF-L was grown on the facet of the 1D ZIF-67 rods, forming 1D ZIF67-rod-embedded Zn-based ZIF-L (hereafter, ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods). The combination of ZIF-67 rods and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based ZIF-L induced higher $\mathrm{CPO}_{(200)/(004)}$ (0.57) and $\mathrm{CPO}_{(020)/(004)}$ (0.7) compared with those of pristine ZIF-L (0.362 and 0.41, respectively) (Figure S2 of the Supporting Information).
Subsequent calcination resulted in the formation of a daisylike structure consisting of the heterogeneous $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-}Z n O}$ composite (Figure 3a).
Interestingly, the numbers of ZIF-67 rods anchored to Znbased ZIF-L were manipulated by using a different concentration of ZIF-67 rods dispersed in EtOH. For example, the single-ZIF-67-rod-loaded Zn-based ZIF-L was synthesized from 1.2 wt $\%$ of a ZIF-67 rod dispersed solution (Figure 3b), while the numbers of both ZIF-67 rods and $Z\mathbf{n}$ -based ZIF-L in hierarchical ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods were increased in the case of using 3.5 or 7 wt $\%$ of ZIF-67 rod dispersed solution (Figure 3c,d). Numerous ZIF-67 rods are embedded within multiply assembled thin-shelled $Z\mathbf{n}$ -based ZIF-L. Hereafter, each ZIF$\mathrm{L}(\varpi Z\mathrm{IF}{-}67\$ rod corresponding to Figure $36{\-}{-}\mathrm{d}$ are denoted as single-ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods, double-ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods, and triple-ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods. Their corresponding SEM images clearly show a different density of ZIF-67 rods on the ZIF-L (inset SEM images of Figure $3\mathbf{b-d}$ ). The as-prepared singleZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods, double-ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods, and tripleZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods were calcined to produce heterogeneous $\mathrm{ZnO-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composites. As shown in Figure 3e, after calcination at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\mathrm{~1~h,~}}$ the single- ${\cdot}{\bf C}_{}{\circ}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}$ -rod-loaded $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet (single- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle({a})\sum\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet) was successfully formed. Similarly, double-ZIF-L@ZIF-67 rods and triple-ZIFL@ZIF-67 rods were oxidized to $\mathrm{ZnO-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composite (hereafter, double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet and triple- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a)\sum\mathrm{nO}$ sheet) while maintaining their hierarchical nanostructures (Figure $^{3\mathrm{f},\mathbf{g}}.$ ).
As shown in the XRD pattern of Figure 4a, three calcined single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet, double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle({a})\sum\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet, and triple- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\mathtt{\widehat{a}m O}$ sheet structures showed hexagonal $\mathrm{{znO}}$ crystallinity. The PXRD analysis of pristine ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods and the pristine $\mathrm{znO}$ sheet was also carried out as reference samples. They showed characteristic XRD peaks of ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ (JCPDS 42-1467) and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ (JCPDS 89-0511). However, in the case of both the single- ${\mathrm{.Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet and double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet, the characteristic peaks of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ phase were not observed because of the low loading amounts of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . On the other hand, in the case of the triple- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\mathtt{@Z n O}$ sheet, blurred XRD peaks were observed because of the overlap of characteristic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ peaks of (311) and (220) with $\mathrm{znO}$ peaks. To confirm the amounts of Co contents in each $\mathrm{Co}3\mathrm{O}4$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet composite, we carried out ICP-OES (inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy) analysis by using the single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\mathtt{\textregistered Z n O}$ sheet, double- ${\mathrm{.Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet, and triple${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet.
As a result, single- ${\cdot}{\bf C}{\circ}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}$ $\mathrm{NRs}\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ nanosheets and the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet showed relatively low Co contents ( $0.27\%$ and $0.71\%$ ) in composite structures, while triple- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $\mathrm{NRs}\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ nanosheets showed the highest Co contents $\left(1.82\%\right)$ (Table S3 of the Supporting Information). As evidence for the coexistence of $\mathrm{znO}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ energydispersive $\mathrm{x}$ -ray spectrometer (EDS) mapping results showed a homogeneous distribution of the Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ components in all composite oxide structures (Figure 4b and Figure S9 of the Supporting Information). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis also confirmed the presence of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2\bar{+}}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}.$ , and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ phases in the triple- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a\mathbf{)}2\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet (Figure 4d,e) as well as in both the single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a\mathbf{)}2\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet and double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet (Figure S10 of the Supporting Information). Furthermore, the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern result is well-matched with XRD results (Figure 4c).
To investigate the potential feasibility of heterogeneous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheets as a gas-sensing layer, we carried out gas-sensing tests toward seven different gas species, i.e., hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ , acetone ( $\mathrm{{'}C H_{3}C O C H_{3})}$ , ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ , carbon monoxide (CO), nitric monoxide (NO), formaldehyde (HCHO), and toluene $\left(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)$ under $90\%$ relative humidity (RH) using a homemade system.17
As shown in Figure 5a, the double- ${\cdot}{\bf C}_{{\bf0}_{3}}{\bf O}_{4}$ -rods $\mathtt{\textregistered Z n O}$ -sheetbased sensor showed the highest acetone response at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}=29\right.$ at $5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone, where $R_{\mathrm{air}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{gas}}$ are the resistances of sensing layers in air and target gases, respectively) compared with those of single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{\bar{O}}_{4}$ rods $@$ $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheets $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}\right.=\:12.5$ at $5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone) and triple$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheets $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}=23\right.$ at 5 ppm acetone). It is noted that we selected $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as the optimized sensing temperature on the basis of the temperature-dependent acetone-sensing response values (Figure S11 of the Supporting Information). In addition, heterogeneous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a)\sum\mathrm{nO}$ sheets showed a much improved acetone response compared to the single $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ sheet $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}=10\right.$ at 5 ppm acetone) and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}=1.06\right$ at $5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone). In terms of selectivity, double- $\bar{\mathbf{C}}\mathbf{o}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheets exhibited outstanding acetone selectivity against interfering analytes $(R_{\mathrm{air}}/{\hbar}$ $R_{\mathrm{gas}}<15\AA$ , Figure 5b). The theoretical detection limit of the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\mathtt{\textregistered Z n O}$ sheets was calculated by an approximated linear plot as a function of acetone concentration, considering the noise level of the base resistances (Figure S12 of the Supporting Information).27 The detection limit of double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet level is 5 ppb level acetone concentration with apparent response $(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}~=$ 1.04). Although the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods@ZnO sheets also showed a relatively high response toward HCHO $(R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{gas}}=$ 15), the selectivity toward the two gases can be clearly distinguished using the sensor arrays. To further improve the acetone selectivity of our sensor arrays, i.e., single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods@ZnO sheets, double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheets, and triple- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle({a})\sum\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheets, we carried out principal component analysis (PCA), which is widely used as a pattern recognition tool.28 As a result, the 6 interfering gas molecules and acetone gas were clearly classified to two clusters (red dotted region and green dotted regions) even at a highly humid state $(90\mathrm{RH\%})$ in the 2D space (Figure 5c). According to previous studies, ppm level acetone gas molecules in human breath, having a high humidity level $\left({>90}\ \mathrm{RH\%}\right)$ , is strongly related with diabetes and fat-burning. Thus, our HMOFderived heterogeneous sensing layers including single- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods@ZnO sheets, double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheets, and triple- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheets can be potentially applied to a breath analyzer for human health care.
The improved acetone-sensing characteristics are attributed to the (i) creation of $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterointerfaces, (ii) catalytic effect of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods on 2D $\mathrm{znO}$ sheet, and (iii) highly porous nanostructures. First, $\mathtt{p-n}$ junctions are formed at the interface between p-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods and the $\mathbf{n}$ -type $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet (blue ellipses in Figure 5d); the $\mathtt{p-n}$ junctions resulted in the formation of a larger electron depletion region, leading to sensitive resistance variation when target gases are exposed to sensing layers. Thus, the thickness of the electron depletion region formed at the interface between ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods and the $\mathrm{znO}$ sheet can be hugely modulated (thinned), inducing a rapid decrease of resistance. The higher base resistance of the double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle({a})\sum\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet $(8\bar{\mathbf{M}}\Omega)$ compared with those of pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\left(2.08\mathrm{k}\Omega\right)$ and the $\mathrm{znO}$ sheet (6 MΩ) supports that the $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunctions were built between ptype ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods and the $\mathbf{n}$ -type $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet (Figure S9 of the Supporting Information). Furthermore, the baseline resistance of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet composite structures is proportional to the amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet composite because of the large number of $\mathtt{p-n}$ junctions between p-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods and the $\mathfrak{n}$ -type $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet (Figure S13 of the Supporting Information).
Second, catalytic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods immobilized on the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet possess many oxygen vacancies which are inherently generated in spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structure. The multivalence nature of the Co 1 $\mathrm{~\'~}\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{2+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ ) component in spinel ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ induces the generation of oxygen vacancies on the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ surface,29 leading to enhanced gas reaction sites (red ellipses in Figure 5d). Furthermore, catalytic ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ is a widely known effective material for selective activation of the reaction between acetone and chemisorbed oxygen species when introduced on metal oxides.28,30−32 Since the sensing reaction is based on the chemical interaction between acetone molecules and chemisorbed oxygen species as expressed in eq 1, a larger amount of chemisorbed oxygen $(\mathrm{O}^{-})$ species adsorbed on the sensing layers are advantageous to improve the sensitivity.16 As shown in the XPS spectra of the double- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet (Figure 5e), the peaks of chemisorbed oxygen $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\omega}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{O}^{2-})$ were clearly observed at binding energies of 530.2 and $531.0\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively.33 The ratio of $\mathrm{~\bar{~}O^{-}/O^{\frac{\d}{2}-}~}$ (1.203) in the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet was much higher than those of the single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a)\sum\mathrm{nO}$ sheet (0.615) and triple$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet (0.727) (Table S4 and Figure S14 of the Supporting Information). On the basis of the peak ratio of chemisorbed oxygen in the sensing layers, the double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheets are an optimized acetone-sensing layer among different $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -loaded $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet composites.
