## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we synthesized variously shaped ZIF-67 and ZIF8, including polyhedron, sheet, rod, and belt, by controlling the relative ratio of two mixed solvents, EtOH and DI water. Heterogeneous 1D $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -rod-loaded 2D $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ sheets were achieved via in situ coupling of sheet-shaped $Z\mathbf{n}$ -based ZIF-L and rod-shaped ZIF-67 followed by high-temperature calcination. Because of the (i) heterojunction $\mathtt{p-n}$ junction effect, (ii) catalytic effect of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ and (iii) porous structure of hybridMOF-templated $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\scriptstyle(a\mathbf{)}2\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}$ sheet, significantly enhanced acetone-sensing properties were achieved. With this strategy, one can rationally design the desired heterogeneous composite structures with diverse MOF morphologies, which are highly suitable not only for chemical sensors but also for other applications such as catalysis and energy storage.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{(Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}$ , zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , 2-methylimidazole (HMIM, ${\mathrm{C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{N}}_{2},$ $99.0\%$ ), and absolute ethanol 1 $\mathrm{^CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ , EtOH) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO). All chemicals were used without further purification. No unexpected or unusually high safety hazards were encountered in any of the experimental procedures.
Synthesis of Polyhedral ZIF-67, Co-Based ZIF-L, ZIF67 Rods, and ZIF-67 Belts. First, conventional polyhedral ZIF-67 was synthesized by using methanol solvent. A $\mathbf{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ portion of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate and $\mathbf{0.65\g}$ of HMIM were separately dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol to prepare two solutions (Co-ion-dissolved methanol and HMIM-dissolved methanol). The two as-prepared solutions were mixed and vigorously stirred for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. Afterward, the obtained purple solution was purified two times by centrifugation $3000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\ \mathrm{\min},$ ). Then, the obtained sediment was dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Second, nanosheetshaped ZIF-L was prepared in DI water. Similarly, $\boldsymbol{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate and $\mathbf{0.65}~\mathrm{~g~}$ of HMIM were separately dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water. The two solutions were mixed and stirred for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. The washing and collection processes of ZIF-L were conducted in the same condition. Lastly, ZIF-67 rods and ZIF-67 belts were synthesized by using the mixed cosolvent of $\mathrm{EtOH}$ and DI water. In the case of ZIF-67 rods, $\mathbf{0.3~g}$ of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate was dissolved in the $27~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water while 0.65 $\mathbf{g}$ of HMIM was dissolved in $13~\mathrm{mL}$ of EtOH. Afterward, HMIM-dissolved EtOH solution was gently poured into the cobalt-nitrate-hexahydrate-dissolved DI water and stirred for 3 $\mathbf{h}$ at room temperature. In the case of ZIF-67 belts, $_{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate was dissolved in $13~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water while $0.65\mathrm{\g}$ of HMIM was dissolved in $27~\mathrm{mL}$ of EtOH. Then, the EtOH-based solution was also poured into the DI-waterbased solution and stirred for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. It is noted that the washing and collection processes of ZIF-67 rods and ZIF-67 belts were also the same as in the above method.
Synthesis of HMOF and HMOF-Driven Heterogeneous $Z n O/C o_{3}O_{4}$ Hierarchical Structures. Solutions of 1.2, 3.5, and 7 wt $\%$ of as-prepared ZIF-67 rods were dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water, respectively. Then, $_{0.3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate was dissolved in DI water solution including the as-prepared ZIF-67 rods. Meanwhile, $0.65\mathrm{~g~}$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water. The two solutions were mixed and gently stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ . The asprepared lilac solution was purified by centrifugation at 3000 rpm for $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{{min}}}$ . Then, the lilac sediment was washed by EtOH and fully dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . To achieve the heterogeneous 1D $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -rod-embedded 2D $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet, which includes the single- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet, double- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet, and triple- ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nO}$ sheet, three lilac powders having different concentrations of ZIF-67 rods were calcined at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ with a ramping rate $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . Three different powders showed yellow−green colors with different brightness, having hierarchical $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composite structures.
Material Characterization. The morphologies of each sample were examined by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, Nova 230). The microstructure, selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern, and EDS mapping analysis of each sample were analyzed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Tecnai F30 S-Twin, FEI). The crystal structure of each sample was observed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern with an X-ray diffractometer $\mathrm{{(D/}}$ MAX-RC 12 kW, Rigaku) using $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ $\dot{(\lambda}=1.54\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ radiation. The chemical bonding states of Co, Zn, and $\mathrm{~o~}$ in HMOFderived metal oxide were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Sigma Probe, Thermo VG Scientific) with Al $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation $\cdot1486.6~\mathrm{eV})$ . Sensing properties of the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sheet, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods, single- ${\mathrm{.Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet, double$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ sheet, and triple- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ rods $\ @\mathrm{ZnO}$ sheet were evaluated by homemade testing equipment described elsewhere. The resistance variation of each sample was measured by using a data acquisition system (34972A, Agilent).
